Lab Exercise 1: Foundations of Information Systems
A. Lab Objectives
By the end of this lab, students will be able to:
1.	Define an Information System and identify its purpose.
2.	Analyze how IS supports business activities.
3.	Identify and describe components of an IS.
4.	Classify different types of information systems.
5.	Evaluate system effectiveness and efficiency.
B. Lab Activities
1. Introduction to Information System (IS)
Task 1: Identify Real-World Information Systems
List five information systems you use in daily life. For each system, write:
•	System Name
•	Purpose
•	Users
•	Information processed
Example:
System	Purpose	Users	Information
ATM System	Cash withdrawal	Public	Account balance
			


2. Role of IS in Business
Task 2: Case Study Analysis
Choose any sector (Hospital, Hospitality, Banking, Retail, Transportation, Education) and complete:
1.	Identify five business problems in that sector.
2.	Describe how an information system can solve each problem.

3. Components of Information System
Task 3: IS Components Identification
For any familiar system (e.g., Online Shopping, Student Record System), identify the 5 components:
•	Hardware used
•	Software used
•	Data processed
•	People involved
•	Procedures followed
Create a table:
Component	Description
Hardware	Barcode scanner, server, etc.

4. Types of Information Systems
Task 4: Classify IS Types
For each type, provide a real example and describe its function:
1.	Transaction Processing System (TPS)
2.	Management Information System (MIS)
3.	Decision Support System (DSS)
4.	Executive Support System (ESS)
5.	Office Automation System (OAS)
6.	Knowledge Management System (KMS)
7.	Example:
•	TPS: POS billing system—records sales transactions.
5. Effectiveness and Efficiency in IS
Task 5: Evaluate a Real System
Choose one system used in your college (e.g., attendance system, library management, exam portal).
Evaluate it based on the following:
Efficiency Criteria:
•	Speed
•	Accuracy
•	Cost
•	Resource usage
•	Response time
Effectiveness Criteria:
•	Ease of use
•	Reliability
•	User satisfaction
•	Flexibility
•	Information quality
Rate each item from 1 (Poor) to 5 (Excellent).
Create a table:
Criteria	Score (1–5)	Remarks
Finally, write three improvements that could make the system more effective or efficient.


